From a6b9761d39a5feee08fc46006b3bcc1e19dbcb36 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: AmirSalahY <amir@noury.ch>
Date: Sun, 2 Aug 2026 18:26:19 +0300
Subject: [PATCH 14/21] feat(ui): tint each row by what it is
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

A folder, a file and a subcommand read as one undifferentiated list when every
row shares a foreground colour. Rows are now tinted by kind: systemTeal folders,
systemGreen subcommands, systemPurple options, grey arguments, files left at the
baseline. The palette follows what a terminal has meant for decades — `ls`
colours directories blue and executables green — so it reads correctly before it
is explained. Teal rather than blue for folders because the selection bar is
already systemBlue, and a blue label on a blue bar loses information.

Only the label is tinted; the icon column keeps its dim grey. The glyph already
encodes the kind by shape, and colouring both read as two competing highlights
per row. It also means the tint still works under icons = "none", where colour
becomes the only cue.

This needed a correctness fix first. Suggestion.type was copied straight off the
spec's suggestion object, where it is almost never set — the real kind arrives as
a separate contextual argument, which is what the ICON already resolved from. So
nearly every row carried type: undefined and colouring by it would have painted
one flat colour. Both now derive from the same expression, which is what keeps
the tint and the glyph agreeing; the 43 changed snapshot lines are that field
becoming populated.

Off via theme.colorByType, overridable per kind under [theme.types], and
reported by `doctor` with a warning when the terminal is below truecolour and
the hexes get quantised.
